Thanks Mathieu, the design of both halves held up under review: the identity probes all came back clean (missing/empty/non-string identifier claims fail closed in both sources, the Section 5.3.2 sub match still fires with a custom identifier claim, and the provider keys the user on the validated identifier). I fixed four things while preparing the branch and force-pushed (d9209228e52, your two commits kept):

- The claims-source commit missed its own use case: the discovery service still required `userinfo_endpoint` to be announced, so `user_data_source: id_token` against a provider without one failed with "The userinfo_endpoint is missing from the OIDC discovery document". The factory now only requires that endpoint when it is the claims source; the userinfo path still fails cleanly at runtime when the endpoint is absent.
- The end-session listener ran at priority 0 and set its response unconditionally, so it silently replaced a custom logout listener's response and could drop the `Clear-Site-Data` header depending on registration order. It now runs at priority 65 (just above the default listener) and only acts when no earlier listener set a response.
- An `end_session_endpoint` carrying a query string (e.g. `?tenant=acme`) got a second `?`; the listener now appends with the same separator logic `start()` uses.
- Polish: dead discovery call removed, catch narrowed to `AuthenticationException`, `user_identifier_claim` turned into a real config node with an info text warning that `sub` is the only claim OIDC guarantees stable and unique, Security/Http changelog entry added.

Kept as one PR: the first commit stands alone, three suites are green at both commits, and both rework the same factory surface, so splitting would only cost a rebase chain. Description updated with the behavioral details.

